CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe pelvic pain, intractable nausea, tachycardia (HR 115), and marked abdominal guarding in the context of extreme medical trauma and care avoidance. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was absolutely critical to perform a modified, trauma-informed abdominal examination to evaluate for immediate life-threatening surgical emergencies (like a ruptured ectopic pregnancy) and to provide urgent, potent analgesia. Delaying assessment until routine hours or forcing a non-consensual ED transfer would have exacerbated the trauma and posed a severe risk to life if organic pathology was actively rupturing, strictly satisfying the criteria for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular ketorolac 30mg and intramuscular ondansetron 4mg to rapidly control the severe pain and vomiting. Wrote a strict, patient-directed referral for a private, outpatient pelvic ultrasound to bypass the emergency department. Advised the usual GP to arrange qualitative assessment highlighting reproductive coercion trauma and to monitor elevated stress biomarkers.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the acute pelvic floor tension safely, implement a "diaphragmatic release breathing" technique: place one hand on the lower stomach, inhale deeply for 4 seconds causing the hand to rise, and exhale for 6 seconds; focusing strictly on the mechanical movement of the breath physically forces the pelvic floor to drop and relax, interrupting the pain cycle. To combat legislative hypervigilance, establish a "news-free sanctuary space" in the bedroom: remove all screens and alerts from this room, making it a physical zone where external socio-political threats cannot enter, thereby improving sleep architecture.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a reproductive justice advocacy organization and a trauma-informed gynecologist.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory telehealth GP review within 24 hours to ensure pain is managed and to confirm the outpatient ultrasound appointment.

